course: dictionary
lesson: 9380
title: One word, more than one job
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words can do more than one job. The clues around the word tell you which job it is doing. «The spotlight found the singer in the middle of the stage.» A spotlight is a strong lamp that shines a narrow, bright beam, so here it is a thing you can see.
explain: The same word can also be a doing word. «The head teacher will spotlight our science project on Monday.» Spotlight is now a verb, and it means to draw special attention to something. Ask yourself: is this word a thing, or is somebody doing something?
explain: Some phrases mean more than their single words. «After her goal, the striker was in the spotlight all week.» Being in the spotlight means getting a lot of attention from people or the news. Read the whole phrase, not just one word, before you decide what it means.
fact: A noun names a thing, and a verb is a doing word.
fact: As a noun, «spotlight» means a strong lamp with a narrow, bright beam, especially on a stage.
fact: As a verb, «spotlight» means to shine a light on something or to draw special attention to it.
fact: «In the spotlight» means getting a lot of attention from people or the news.
fact: In science, a spotlight is an example of light travelling in straight lines and making a bright circle.
exercise: «The spotlight found the dancer as she walked onto the stage.» What is a spotlight here? | A loud noise from the audience | A kind of stage curtain | *A strong lamp that shines a narrow, bright beam | A song the band plays
exercise: Mia says, «Let's spotlight our favourite books in assembly.» What does spotlight mean here? | To hide something away in a box | To sing a song loudly | To lose something on the way | *To draw special attention to something
exercise: «After her goal, the striker was in the spotlight all week.» What does «in the spotlight» mean? | Hiding from the crowd | Feeling cold and tired | Standing under a lamp on a stage | *Getting a lot of attention from people or the news
test: «The Earth turns 360 degrees in 24 hours, so noon slides westwards around the planet like a spotlight.» What does «like a spotlight» suggest? | A loud siren going off | Something that stays in one place forever | *A bright circle of light moving steadily across the ground | A ball bouncing down the stairs
test: Which sentence uses spotlight as a verb? | The spotlight swept across the empty stage. | The lamp in the hall is very bright. | *The class will spotlight the best poem on Friday. | Ella carried the spotlight to the back room.
exam: «The spotlight effect is the feeling that everyone notices you far more than they really do.» What does spotlight effect mean? | A way to make a bright beam of light | Being very tired after a long day | A lamp that shines onto a person on a stage | *The feeling that everyone notices you far more than they really do
